  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Four standard six-sided dice are rolled, one after another, and the resulting list of four numbers is recorded. Find the probability that:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "the list contains no 6.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{625}{1296}$",
          "worked": "The rolls are independent, and each has probability $\\frac56$ of not being a six.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{no six})&=\\left(\\frac56\\right)^4\\\\\\Pr(\\text{no six})&=\\frac{625}{1296}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the list contains at least one 6",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{671}{1296}$\n\nSubtract the probability of no sixes from 1.",
          "worked": "The complementary event is obtaining no sixes.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{at least one six})&=1-\\Pr(\\text{no six})\\\\\\Pr(\\text{at least one six})&=1-\\frac{625}{1296}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{at least one six})&=\\frac{671}{1296}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q14-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "the first number is 6, but none of the other numbers are 6",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{125}{1296}$",
          "worked": "Multiply the probabilities for the four independent rolls.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{first roll only is six})&=\\frac16\\left(\\frac56\\right)^3\\\\\\Pr(\\text{first roll only is six})&=\\frac{125}{1296}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q14-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "the last number is 6, but none of the other numbers are 6",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{125}{1296}$",
          "worked": "Multiply the probabilities for the four independent rolls.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{last roll only is six})&=\\left(\\frac56\\right)^3\\frac16\\\\\\Pr(\\text{last roll only is six})&=\\frac{125}{1296}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q14-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "exactly one of the numbers is 6.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{125}{324}$\n\nAdd the probabilities for the four possible positions of the single six.",
          "worked": "The single six can occur in any of four positions. These cases are mutually exclusive, each with probability $\\frac{125}{1296}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{exactly one six})&=4\\times\\frac{125}{1296}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{exactly one six})&=\\frac{500}{1296}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{exactly one six})&=\\frac{125}{324}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
